// **`$T` polymorphic parameters** (ADR-0081): a procedure whose parameter type is a variable a call
// infers. W5's first sub-wave, and it is deliberately the *surface* only — a `$T` procedure parses,
// formats, lowers as a template and **checks clean when declared**. *Calling* one is refused (E0268) until
// the instantiation sub-wave, which is exercised in `type-errors/066`.
//
// The reason the wave splits here is architectural, and worth stating: instantiation multiplies
// procedures — `id(42)` and `id(true)` become two concrete functions — and the whole compiler currently
// keys a procedure by one `ProcId` per source declaration. Threading a distinct instantiation through
// MIR, the VM and the native back end is the second sub-wave's work. This one delivers everything up to
// that point, so the syntax and the template representation are real and verified before instantiation is
// built on them.
//
// What is checked here:
//
//   * a single `$T` inferred from one parameter (`id`);
//   * a `$T` used across **several** positions — one variable, three uses (`first`, `pair_sum`), which is
//     ADR-0081 §4's "one `$T` across positions" as opposed to two independent variables;
//   * `$T` **nested** in a pointer and a view (`deref`, `count_view`), so the collector's recursion is
//     covered;
//   * `$T` in the **return** type, read as a bare `T` (`id`, `first`), which is what the binding-map
//     lookup in `resolve_type_name` makes work.
//
// A `$T` procedure lowers to **no MIR** — it is a template, skipped exactly as a `#foreign` body is —
// so none of these appears in a MIR snapshot, and that is correct: there is nothing concrete to lower
// until a call binds `T`.
#import "Basic";

/// The canonical example: one `$T`, inferred from `x`, returned as a bare `T`.
id :: (x: $T) -> T {
    return x;
}

/// One `$T` across two parameters and the return — a single variable, three uses, not three variables.
first :: (a: $T, b: T) -> T {
    return a;
}

/// `$T` behind a pointer, so the collector recurses through `*`.
deref :: (p: *$T) -> T {
    return p.*;
}

/// `$T` as a view's element, so the collector recurses through `[]`.
count_view :: (items: []$T) -> s64 {
    return items.count;
}

/// Two parameters of the same `$T`, summed — the shape a later sub-wave will instantiate per type.
pair_sum :: (a: $T, b: T) -> T {
    return a + b;
}

// None of the above is *called*, so `main` does ordinary work: the file's point is that declaring these
// checks clean, which before this sub-wave it did not — `$T` did not even lex.
main :: () {
    n := 5;
    exit(n);
}
